            <div xml:id="d653">

                <pb facs="0753" n="651" sameAs="frus:frus1927v03/pg651"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">361.1121 Kudrasheff, Alexander</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Norway</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Swenson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Oslo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1926-12-27"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">December 27,
                        1926</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received January 24, 1927.]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 918</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to the Department’s instruction
                    No. 298 of August 26, 1926, and to my despatch No. 903 dated November 22, 1926,
                    with regard to the American citizens Kudrasheff, Chevalier, and Kopman,
                    imprisoned in Soviet Russia, I have the honor to report that I am in receipt of
                    the following note from the Norwegian Foreign Office in this matter: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#small-caps">translation</hi></p>
                        <p rendition="#right">Oslo, December 22, 1926.</p>
                        <p rendition="#flush">Royal Foreign Office<lb/>The Undersecretary.</p>
                        <p rendition="#flush">Mr. Minister:</p>
                        <p>With reference to my note of the eighteenth ultimo regarding the three
                            American citizens Kudrasheff, Chevalier, and Kopman, who are imprisoned
                            in Soviet Russia, I have the honor to advise that the Legation in Moscow
                            has privately ascertained that Julius Chevalier has been released and
                            has either already left Soviet Russian territory or will leave in the
                            very near future. An attempt was made to send him out via Latvia, but
                            the Lettish border authorities placed difficulties in the way of this,
                            and therefore it was necessary to send him another way without the
                            Legation hearing by which route. But the case of the man in question is
                            presumably in order.</p>
                        <p>As for the other two, Kudrasheff and Kopman, it appeared certain that
                            they were Russian subjects, so no information regarding them could be
                            given to the Legation in Moscow.</p>
                        <p>Accept, Mr. Minister, the assurance of my distinguished
                            consideration.</p>
                        <p rendition="#right">(signed) Aug. Esmarch</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>An appropriate expression of thanks for the interest shown and assistance
                    rendered in this case by the Norwegian Government has been made to the Foreign
                    Office.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Laurits S.
                    Swenson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
